  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-11",
    "title": "NGC 4449: Small Galaxy, Big Impact",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  Don’t judge a galaxy by its size. Our home galaxy, the  Milky Way , is about 100,000  light years  across. This huge expanse of space contains hundreds of billions of stars! The smallest galaxies, called  dwarf galaxies , are about 1/10th the size of the Milky Way and only contain a few billion stars. They are also the  most common type of galaxy  in the whole universe.  Today’s image  shows one such dwarf galaxy,  NGC 4449 , captured by the  Hubble Space Telescope . NGC 4449 and similar dwarf galaxies are  missing more oxygen  than models predict. This suggests their  stellar winds  and  supernovae  are surprisingly effective at  blowing away  oxygen and other heavy elements. Turns out these tiny galaxies may play a big role in spreading heavy elements — the building blocks of stars, planets, and life — throughout the universe!   Tomorrow's picture:  a date in Poland",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/hubble/galaxies/irregular/NGC%204449.jpg?w=2048&h=1320&fit=clip&crop=faces,focalpoint",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-11-ngc-4449-small-galaxy-big-impact/",
    "credit": "NASA, ESA, A. Aloisi (STScI/ESA), and the Hubble Heritage (STScI/AURA)-ESA/Hubble Collaboration",
    "alt": "A large smear of stars, almost shaped like a bow-tie, covers most of the image. The center of the \"bow\" is full of pink clumps of gas, while the outsides consist of blue-white stars, streaks of dark dust, and more pink globs of gas.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-11T08:50:06.485004+00:00"
